export const PKG_FRONTEND_README = "# NESR SLK Competency Framework System \u2014 standalone build\n\nA ready-to-run packaging of the application as a normal Vite + React project.\nEvery feature built in the Claude artifact version is preserved unchanged \u2014\nthis package only adds the scaffolding needed to run it outside that\nenvironment.\n\n## Two modes, one codebase\n\nThis project can run two ways, switched by one environment variable:\n\n| Mode | `VITE_USE_API` | Storage | Multi-user? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Standalone (default) | unset / `false` | IndexedDB, local to your browser | No \u2014 see the limitations below |\n| Connected | `true` | The real API in `../nesr-wl-api/` | Yes \u2014 real Azure SQL, real Entra auth |\n\nCopy `.env.example` to `.env.local` and set `VITE_USE_API=true` plus\n`VITE_API_BASE_URL` once the API is deployed (see `nesr-wl-api/README.md`) to\nrun in connected mode. Nothing else changes \u2014 same `npm run dev`, same\n`npm run build`.\n\n## Quick start (standalone mode)\n\n```bash\nnpm install\nnpm run dev\n```\n\nOpen the URL Vite prints (usually `http://localhost:5173`). The app loads,\nthe \"Set your name\" gate appears, and you can use every tab exactly as\nbefore.\n\nTo build a deployable static bundle:\n\n```bash\nnpm run build\n```\n\nThis produces a `dist/` folder \u2014 a set of static HTML/JS/CSS files. Upload\nthat folder's contents to any static host (Azure Static Web Apps, Netlify,\nVercel, an S3 bucket, an internal web server) and the app runs from there.\nNo server-side runtime is required to serve it.\n\n## What's actually inside\n\n- `src/App.jsx` \u2014 the application itself, unchanged from the version you've\n  been testing. Every feature is here: all six tabs, dark/light mode,\n  responsive layout, the notification centre, certification lockout, the\n  audit trail, backups, PDF/Excel/PNG export, everything.\n- `src/main.jsx` \u2014 the standard React entry point that mounts the app, and\n  picks which storage backend to load based on `VITE_USE_API`.\n- `src/storagePolyfill.js` \u2014 the standalone, IndexedDB-backed storage. Read\n  this before relying on standalone mode for real work \u2014 explained below.\n- `src/storageApi.js` \u2014 the real, API-backed storage used in connected mode.\n  See `../nesr-wl-api/` for the backend it talks to.\n- `package.json`, `vite.config.js`, `index.html`, `.env.example` \u2014 ordinary\n  Vite project scaffolding.\n\n## The one thing that had to change: storage\n\nThe app was built against `window.storage`, an API Claude's artifact preview\nprovides \u2014 a shared, network-backed key-value store, live across everyone\nusing the artifact. **That API does not exist in a normal browser.** Deployed\nas-is with no changes, the app would fail immediately: every save, every\nlogin, every backup calls `window.storage`, and it would throw\n\"`window.storage is undefined`\" before you even reached the login screen.\n\n`src/storagePolyfill.js` fixes that by implementing the exact same four\nmethods (`get`, `set`, `delete`, `list`) against **IndexedDB**, a standard\nbrowser storage API. It matches the original's method signatures and return\nshapes precisely, so nothing in `App.jsx` had to change \u2014 the app has no idea\nit's talking to a polyfill instead of the real thing.\n\n### What this means in practice\n\nThis package runs the app **fully functional, entirely locally, in one\nbrowser.** Good for:\n\n- Evaluating the app, or demonstrating it to stakeholders\n- A single person using it for their own work\n- Development and testing\n\n**Not** what it gives you:\n\n- **Multi-user sharing.** Two people opening this build in two different\n  browsers each get their own private, unsynchronised dataset. There is no\n  \"team\" \u2014 the app's \"shared cloud storage\" messaging is now describing\n  data that only exists on one machine.\n- **Data survival across \"clear browsing data.\"** IndexedDB is real,\n  persistent storage that survives closing the tab and restarting the\n  browser \u2014 but a person clearing site data, using a private/incognito\n  window, or switching browsers starts from nothing.\n- **A backend that enforces anything.** Every role check, every\n  certification lockout, every access restriction still runs entirely in\n  the browser. Anyone with developer tools open can see and change\n  everything, exactly as in the artifact version.\n\nNone of this is a defect introduced by packaging it \u2014 it's the same\nlimitation the app always had, just now visible outside a sandbox that\nhappened to hide it behind a working shared backend.\n\n## Moving to a real multi-user deployment\n\nThe API is already built \u2014 see `../nesr-wl-api/`. Deploying it and switching\nthis app to connected mode gets you genuine multi-user sharing, real Azure\nSQL persistence, and real Entra ID authentication. Read\n`../nesr-wl-api/README.md` for the deployment steps; once it's live, set\n`VITE_USE_API=true` and `VITE_API_BASE_URL` here (see `.env.example`) and\nnothing else in this project changes.\n\n**One boundary worth understanding before you rely on this for anything\nsensitive.** The connected API stores data the same way this app always has\n\u2014 one JSON blob per key, the same shape `App.jsx` already reads and writes.\nThat gets you real sharing today, but it does **not** get you row-level\nsecurity: the database sees an opaque blob per key, not individual employee\nrows it can filter by who's asking. An Engineer's browser still receives the\nsame full dataset a Manager's does; the UI still decides what to show, the\nsame as it always has. Real database-enforced row-level security requires\nadopting the fully normalized schema already delivered in\n`NESR_WL_Migration_Schema.sql` (sections 1-12) \u2014 which also means rewriting\nhow `App.jsx` fetches data, one entity at a time, instead of one blob per\nscreen. That's real, separate, larger work described in the roadmap\ndocument; the connected API here is the deliberately smaller step that gets\na team working together on real shared data without waiting for it.\n";
export const PKG_MAIN_JSX = "// Install window.storage BEFORE App mounts \u2014 the app calls it inside effects\n// and event handlers, never at module-load time, but doing this first keeps\n// the ordering obviously correct regardless.\n//\n// Which storage backend loads is controlled by VITE_USE_API (see .env.example):\n//   unset / \"false\" -> storagePolyfill.js, IndexedDB, local to this browser\n//   \"true\"          -> storageApi.js, the real shared backend\n// This is the one line that changes to move from standalone demo mode to a\n// real multi-user deployment \u2014 nothing else in this file or in App.jsx.\nif (import.meta.env.VITE_USE_API === \"true\") {\n  await import(\"./storageApi.js\");\n} else {\n  await import(\"./storagePolyfill.js\");\n}\n\nimport React from \"react\";\nimport ReactDOM from \"react-dom/client\";\nimport App from \"./App.jsx\";\n\n// No <React.StrictMode> wrapper: it double-invokes effects in dev mode to\n// surface side-effect bugs, and this app's timers/intervals/ref-guards were\n// built and tested only against Claude's artifact environment, which does\n// not run StrictMode. Rather than risk a dev-only double-fire (an extra\n// backup snapshot, a duplicated timer) that never happens in the real\n// production build, StrictMode is left out entirely.\nReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById(\"root\")).render(<App />);\n\n\n";
export const PKG_STORAGE_POLYFILL = "/* ============================================================================\n   window.storage polyfill \u2014 IndexedDB-backed\n   ============================================================================\n   The app was built to run inside Claude's artifact preview, which provides a\n   network-backed `window.storage` key-value API shared across every user of\n   the artifact in real time. That API does not exist in a normal browser \u2014\n   deployed as-is, every one of the app's 26 window.storage calls would throw\n   \"window.storage is undefined\" and the app would fail before the login\n   screen even renders.\n\n   This polyfill implements the exact same method signatures \u2014 get, set,\n   delete, list \u2014 against IndexedDB, so nothing in App.jsx needs to change.\n   It is what makes \"npm run dev\" actually work standalone, today, with zero\n   other infrastructure.\n\n   WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO \u2014 read this before relying on it for real use:\n\n   - IT IS LOCAL TO ONE BROWSER. Data lives in this browser's IndexedDB only.\n     Two people opening this app in two different browsers see two entirely\n     separate, unsynchronised datasets. There is no \"team\" in this polyfill \u2014\n     every save is private to whoever is looking at it. The `shared` parameter\n     is accepted (for API compatibility) but has no effect here, since there\n     is no second party to share with locally.\n   - IT DOES NOT SURVIVE \"clear browsing data\". IndexedDB is real, persistent\n     browser storage \u2014 it survives closing the tab and restarting the browser\n     \u2014 but a person clearing their browser's site data, using a private/\n     incognito window, or switching browsers starts with nothing.\n   - THIS IS A DEMO/EVALUATION MODE, NOT PRODUCTION PERSISTENCE. For an actual\n     multi-user deployment where a team shares one live dataset, this needs to\n     be replaced with real calls to a backend API \u2014 see\n     NESR_WL_Migration_Schema.sql and the Strategic Roadmap document for what\n     that backend looks like (Azure SQL, real auth, row-level security). This\n     polyfill exists so the application is genuinely runnable and demonstrable\n     today, not as a substitute for that.\n\n   Swapping this out later requires touching exactly one file: this one. Every\n   call site in App.jsx stays identical \u2014 replace the IndexedDB calls below\n   with fetch() calls to your real API, keep the same method names and return\n   shapes, and the rest of the app does not need to know anything changed.\n   ============================================================================ */\n\nconst DB_NAME = \"nesr_wl_local_storage\";\nconst DB_VERSION = 1;\nconst STORE_NAME = \"kv\";\n\nlet dbPromise = null;\nfunction openDb() {\n  if (dbPromise) return dbPromise;\n  dbPromise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    const req = indexedDB.open(DB_NAME, DB_VERSION);\n    req.onupgradeneeded = () => {\n      const db = req.result;\n      if (!db.objectStoreNames.contains(STORE_NAME)) db.createObjectStore(STORE_NAME);\n    };\n    req.onsuccess = () => resolve(req.result);\n    req.onerror = () => reject(req.error);\n  });\n  return dbPromise;\n}\n\nfunction tx(mode) {\n  return openDb().then((db) => db.transaction(STORE_NAME, mode).objectStore(STORE_NAME));\n}\n\nconst storage = {\n  async get(key, shared = false) {\n    const store = await tx(\"readonly\");\n    const value = await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n      const req = store.get(key);\n      req.onsuccess = () => resolve(req.result);\n      req.onerror = () => reject(req.error);\n    });\n    if (value === undefined) return null;\n    return { key, value, shared };\n  },\n\n  async set(key, value, shared = false) {\n    const store = await tx(\"readwrite\");\n    await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n      const req = store.put(value, key);\n      req.onsuccess = () => resolve();\n      req.onerror = () => reject(req.error);\n    });\n    return { key, value, shared };\n  },\n\n  async delete(key, shared = false) {\n    const store = await tx(\"readwrite\");\n    await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n      const req = store.delete(key);\n      req.onsuccess = () => resolve();\n      req.onerror = () => reject(req.error);\n    });\n    return { key, deleted: true, shared };\n  },\n\n  async list(prefix = \"\", shared = false) {\n    const store = await tx(\"readonly\");\n    const keys = await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n      const req = store.getAllKeys();\n      req.onsuccess = () => resolve(req.result || []);\n      req.onerror = () => reject(req.error);\n    });\n    const filtered = prefix ? keys.filter((k) => String(k).startsWith(prefix)) : keys;\n    return { keys: filtered, prefix, shared };\n  },\n};\n\nif (typeof window !== \"undefined\" && !window.storage) {\n  window.storage = storage;\n}\n\nexport default storage;\n";
export const PKG_STORAGE_API = "/* ============================================================================\n   window.storage \u2014 real API-backed implementation\n   ============================================================================\n   This is the file described in the roadmap and the standalone package's\n   README as \"the one file that changes\": it implements the exact same\n   get/set/delete/list contract as src/storagePolyfill.js, so nothing else in\n   App.jsx needs to know which one is installed. Swap the import in\n   src/main.jsx from storagePolyfill.js to this file, set VITE_API_BASE_URL,\n   and the app is talking to the real shared backend instead of the local\n   IndexedDB copy.\n\n   Requests rely on the browser having already been authenticated by Static\n   Web Apps' Entra ID login \u2014 no token handling happens here, because SWA\n   attaches the verified identity to every request before it reaches the\n   API (see nesr-wl-api/src/auth.js on the server side). If a request comes\n   back 401, the person isn't signed in; redirecting to SWA's own /.auth/\n   login/aad path is the correct recovery, not retrying the call. */\n\nconst API_BASE = import.meta.env.VITE_API_BASE_URL || \"/api\";\n\nasync function apiCall(path, options = {}) {\n  const res = await fetch(`${API_BASE}${path}`, {\n    ...options,\n    credentials: \"include\", // carries the SWA auth cookie\n    headers: { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\", ...(options.headers || {}) },\n  });\n  if (res.status === 401) {\n    throw new Error(\"Not signed in. Refresh the page to sign in again.\");\n  }\n  if (!res.ok) {\n    let detail = \"\";\n    try { detail = (await res.json())?.error || \"\"; } catch (e) {}\n    throw new Error(detail || `Storage request failed (${res.status}).`);\n  }\n  return res.json();\n}\n\nconst storage = {\n  async get(key, shared = false) {\n    return apiCall(`/storage/${encodeURIComponent(key)}?shared=${shared}`, { method: \"GET\" });\n  },\n\n  async set(key, value, shared = false) {\n    return apiCall(`/storage/${encodeURIComponent(key)}?shared=${shared}`, {\n      method: \"PUT\",\n      body: JSON.stringify({ value }),\n    });\n  },\n\n  async delete(key, shared = false) {\n    return apiCall(`/storage/${encodeURIComponent(key)}?shared=${shared}`, { method: \"DELETE\" });\n  },\n\n  async list(prefix = \"\", shared = false) {\n    return apiCall(`/storage?prefix=${encodeURIComponent(prefix)}&shared=${shared}`, { method: \"GET\" });\n  },\n};\n\nif (typeof window !== \"undefined\") {\n  window.storage = storage;\n}\n\nexport default storage;\n";
export const PKG_NVMRC = "20\n";
export const PKG_API_PACKAGE = "{\n  \"name\": \"nesr-wl-api\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"main\": \"src/functions/*.js\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"start\": \"func start\",\n    \"test\": \"node --test test/\"\n  },\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"@azure/functions\": \"^4.5.1\",\n    \"mssql\": \"^11.0.1\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"azure-functions-core-tools\": \"^4.0.5907\"\n  }\n}\n";
export const PKG_API_HOST = "{\n  \"version\": \"2.0\",\n  \"logging\": {\n    \"applicationInsights\": {\n      \"samplingSettings\": { \"isEnabled\": true, \"excludedTypes\": \"Request\" }\n    }\n  },\n  \"extensionBundle\": {\n    \"id\": \"Microsoft.Azure.Functions.ExtensionBundle\",\n    \"version\": \"[4.*, 5.0.0)\"\n  }\n}\n";
export const PKG_API_LOCAL_SETTINGS = "{\n  \"IsEncrypted\": false,\n  \"Values\": {\n    \"AzureWebJobsStorage\": \"UseDevelopmentStorage=true\",\n    \"FUNCTIONS_WORKER_RUNTIME\": \"node\",\n\n    \"SQL_SERVER\": \"your-server.database.windows.net\",\n    \"SQL_DATABASE\": \"nesr_wl_competency\",\n    \"SQL_AUTH_TYPE\": \"azure-active-directory-default\",\n\n    \"_comment_auth\": \"SQL_AUTH_TYPE=azure-active-directory-default lets the Function App connect using its own Managed Identity \u2014 no password stored anywhere. Grant that identity db_datareader/db_datawriter on the database. Do not switch this to SQL login + password unless there is a specific reason Managed Identity cannot be used.\"\n  },\n  \"Host\": {\n    \"CORS\": \"*\",\n    \"_comment_cors\": \"Tighten this to the Static Web App's actual origin before going anywhere near production. * is only acceptable for local development against a local Vite dev server.\"\n  }\n}\n";
export const PKG_API_README = "# NESR SLK Competency Framework System \u2014 API\n\nThe backend that turns the standalone app (`../nesr-wl-competency-app/`) into\na real, shared, multi-user system. Four HTTP endpoints implementing the exact\n`get`/`set`/`delete`/`list` contract the app already speaks \u2014 see\n`../nesr-wl-competency-app/src/storageApi.js` for the client side of this.\n\n## What this is, and what it deliberately is not\n\nThis is the **key-value tier** \u2014 real Azure SQL persistence and real Entra ID\nauthentication, using the same one-blob-per-key model the app has always\nused, just stored in a shared database instead of a local browser. That is\nenough for genuine multi-user sharing.\n\nIt is **not** the fully normalized, row-level-secured model described in\n`NESR_WL_Migration_Schema.sql` sections 1-12 and the Strategic Roadmap. That\nrequires a larger, separate migration \u2014 rewriting how the app fetches data,\none entity at a time, instead of one JSON blob per screen. Don't let that\nblock getting a team onto real shared data today; this tier exists precisely\nso it doesn't have to.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- An Azure subscription with permission to create resources\n- Azure SQL Database (existing, or create one \u2014 see below)\n- An Entra ID app registration for authentication (your IT/identity team; see\n  \"Setting up Entra auth\" below \u2014 this is the one step that needs someone\n  outside this project)\n- Azure CLI (`az`) installed, or the Azure Portal if you prefer clicking\n  through it\n\n## 1 \u2014 Database setup\n\nRun the base schema and this API's key-value table against your Azure SQL\nDatabase, in order:\n\n```bash\nsqlcmd -S your-server.database.windows.net -d nesr_wl_competency -G \\\n  -i ../NESR_WL_Migration_Schema.sql\n```\n\n`-G` uses Azure AD authentication for the `sqlcmd` session itself \u2014 adjust if\nyour setup uses a different auth method. The file runs the full schema\n(sections 1-13); this API only touches `dbo.KeyValueStore` (section 13), but\nthe whole file is meant to be applied together since later sections\nreference earlier ones.\n\n## 2 \u2014 Deploy the Function App\n\n```bash\naz group create --name nesr-wl-rg --location eastus\n\naz functionapp create \\\n  --resource-group nesr-wl-rg \\\n  --consumption-plan-location eastus \\\n  --runtime node --runtime-version 20 --functions-version 4 \\\n  --name nesr-wl-api --storage-account <your-storage-account>\n\n# Managed Identity \u2014 no password ever stored anywhere\naz functionapp identity assign --name nesr-wl-api --resource-group nesr-wl-rg\n\naz functionapp config appsettings set --name nesr-wl-api --resource-group nesr-wl-rg \\\n  --settings SQL_SERVER=\"your-server.database.windows.net\" \\\n             SQL_DATABASE=\"nesr_wl_competency\"\n\nfunc azure functionapp publish nesr-wl-api\n```\n\n## 3 \u2014 Grant the Function App's identity access to the database\n\nThe Function App now has a Managed Identity (an Azure-managed identity with\nno password to leak). Grant it read/write on the database \u2014 run this against\nyour Azure SQL Database as an Azure AD admin:\n\n```sql\nCREATE USER [nesr-wl-api] FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER;\nALTER ROLE db_datareader ADD MEMBER [nesr-wl-api];\nALTER ROLE db_datawriter ADD MEMBER [nesr-wl-api];\n```\n\n`nesr-wl-api` here is the Function App's name from step 2 \u2014 Azure SQL\nrecognises it as the display name of that Managed Identity automatically.\n\n## 4 \u2014 Setting up Entra auth\n\nThis is the one step that needs someone with organisational authority, not\njust engineering time \u2014 an Entra ID app registration.\n\n1. In Entra ID \u2192 App registrations, register a new application for this\n   system (redirect URI: `https://<your-static-web-app>.azurestaticapps.net/.auth/login/aad/callback`).\n2. Note the Application (client) ID and create a client secret.\n3. Link this to your Static Web App: Portal \u2192 your Static Web App \u2192\n   Authentication \u2192 configure the Azure Active Directory provider with that\n   client ID/secret, or set `identityProviders.azureActiveDirectory` in the\n   frontend's `staticwebapp.config.json` (already scaffolded with the\n   `/api/*` route requiring `authenticated`).\n4. Deploy the Function App **linked to the Static Web App** (either via the\n   SWA \"bring your own Functions\" pairing at deployment, or an API\n   connection in the Portal) so `x-ms-client-principal` gets attached to\n   every request that reaches it \u2014 see `src/auth.js` for how this API reads\n   that header. Requests that reach this API any other way (direct calls,\n   bypassing SWA) will correctly get rejected as unauthenticated, since\n   there's no principal header to read.\n\n## Local development\n\n```bash\ncp local.settings.json.example local.settings.json\n# edit local.settings.json with your dev database details\nnpm install\nfunc start\n```\n\nThere's no SWA proxy locally, so there's no `x-ms-client-principal` header to\nread. Set `NESR_ALLOW_DEV_AUTH=true` in `local.settings.json` and send an\n`x-dev-user` header with your test requests to simulate a signed-in user \u2014\n**read the security warning at the bottom of `src/auth.js` before ever\nsetting that anywhere reachable outside your own machine.**\n\n## A note on what \"multi-user\" means here\n\nEvery value in `dbo.KeyValueStore` is one shared row per key \u2014 the same\nmodel the app has always used, just centralised. Personal settings (theme,\ndisplay name) are namespaced server-side by the caller's verified Entra\nidentity so they don't collide across users (see the comment in\n`src/functions/storage.js`) \u2014 that's a real fix this API makes, not\nsomething the original artifact version or the standalone package needed to\nhandle, since both only ever had one user per browser.\n";
export const PKG_API_DB = "/* ============================================================================\n   Database connection \u2014 one pool, reused across invocations\n   ============================================================================\n   Azure Functions on the Node.js runtime can invoke the same module many\n   times within one warm instance. Opening a new SQL connection per request\n   would exhaust the database's connection limit under real load; a\n   module-level pool, created once and awaited by every function, is the\n   standard pattern here.\n\n   Authentication to SQL uses the Function App's own Managed Identity\n   (azure-active-directory-default) rather than a stored password \u2014 nothing\n   secret lives in this file or in application settings. The Managed Identity\n   needs db_datareader and db_datawriter granted on the database once, during\n   setup; that grant is documented in the README, not automated here, since\n   it is a one-time DBA action. */\n\nconst sql = require(\"mssql\");\n\nlet poolPromise = null;\n\nfunction getPool() {\n  if (!poolPromise) {\n    poolPromise = sql.connect({\n      server: process.env.SQL_SERVER,\n      database: process.env.SQL_DATABASE,\n      authentication: { type: \"azure-active-directory-default\" },\n      options: { encrypt: true, trustServerCertificate: false },\n      pool: { max: 10, min: 0, idleTimeoutMillis: 30000 },\n    }).catch((err) => {\n      // Do not cache a failed connection attempt \u2014 the next call should try\n      // again rather than keep returning the same rejected promise forever.\n      poolPromise = null;\n      throw err;\n    });\n  }\n  return poolPromise;\n}\n\nmodule.exports = { sql, getPool };\n";
export const PKG_API_AUTH = "/* ============================================================================\n   Auth \u2014 reading the identity Static Web Apps already verified\n   ============================================================================\n   When this API is deployed behind an Azure Static Web App with Entra ID\n   configured as the built-in auth provider, every request that reaches this\n   function has ALREADY been authenticated by the platform before it arrives\n   here. SWA injects an `x-ms-client-principal` header \u2014 base64-encoded JSON \u2014\n   containing the caller's verified identity. This function's job is only to\n   read that header, never to perform authentication itself; SWA has already\n   done the actual work of validating the Entra token.\n\n   Local development has no SWA proxy in front of it, so there is no header\n   to read. `x-dev-user` is accepted ONLY as a local-development fallback \u2014\n   see the warning below before this ever runs anywhere reachable from the\n   internet. */\n\nfunction getCallerIdentity(request) {\n  const principalHeader = request.headers.get(\"x-ms-client-principal\");\n  if (principalHeader) {\n    try {\n      const decoded = Buffer.from(principalHeader, \"base64\").toString(\"utf-8\");\n      const principal = JSON.parse(decoded);\n      // userDetails is typically the UPN/email for Entra ID logins.\n      if (principal?.userDetails) return { identity: principal.userDetails, verified: true };\n    } catch (e) {\n      // Malformed header \u2014 treat as unauthenticated rather than trust a\n      // partially-parsed value.\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Local-dev-only fallback. Real requests through Static Web Apps always\n  // carry x-ms-client-principal once Entra auth is configured \u2014 this branch\n  // exists purely so `func start` works before that's wired up.\n  const devUser = request.headers.get(\"x-dev-user\");\n  if (devUser && process.env.NESR_ALLOW_DEV_AUTH === \"true\") {\n    return { identity: devUser, verified: false };\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/* SECURITY \u2014 read before deploying:\n   NESR_ALLOW_DEV_AUTH must never be set to \"true\" on anything reachable\n   outside a local machine. With it set, anyone can claim to be any user by\n   sending an x-dev-user header \u2014 there is no verification at all on that\n   path. It exists only so the API is runnable locally before Entra/SWA auth\n   is wired up; treat it the same as a debug backdoor, because that is\n   exactly what it is. */\n\nmodule.exports = { getCallerIdentity };\n";
export const PKG_API_STORAGE_FN = "/* ============================================================================\n   Storage endpoints \u2014 get / set / delete / list\n   ============================================================================\n   Implements the exact contract src/storagePolyfill.js already defines in\n   the standalone package, so swapping the app onto this API is the one-file\n   change described in the roadmap: point main.jsx at a fetch()-based client\n   instead of the IndexedDB one, change nothing in App.jsx.\n\n   ONE REAL DIFFERENCE FROM THE POLYFILL, AND WHY IT'S HERE:\n   The app has always called storage with a `shared` flag \u2014 true for team\n   data, false for personal settings like the display name and theme\n   preference. In the IndexedDB polyfill that distinction was cosmetic: every\n   key already lived in one browser, so there was never a second user to\n   collide with. Here there genuinely is one. If a \"personal\" key like\n   nesr_wl_theme_pref were stored under that literal name in a real shared\n   table, every user's request would read and overwrite the SAME row \u2014 the\n   last person to change their theme would silently change everyone's.\n\n   The fix: personal keys are namespaced server-side by the caller's VERIFIED\n   identity (from src/auth.js \u2014 never a client-supplied value) before\n   touching the database. A client never sees this prefix; get/set/delete\n   still take the same plain key the app already uses, and the personal/\n   shared split behaves correctly without App.jsx knowing anything changed. */\n\nconst { app } = require(\"@azure/functions\");\nconst { sql, getPool } = require(\"../db\");\nconst { getCallerIdentity } = require(\"../auth\");\n\nfunction resolveKey(rawKey, shared, identity) {\n  return shared ? rawKey : `personal:${identity}:${rawKey}`;\n}\n\nfunction requireAuth(request) {\n  const caller = getCallerIdentity(request);\n  if (!caller) return null;\n  return caller;\n}\n\nfunction escapeLikePattern(s) {\n  // % _ and [ are LIKE wildcards in T-SQL \u2014 a prefix like \"nesr_wl_backup_\"\n  // (which the app actually uses) is full of underscores that must be\n  // treated as literal characters, not \"match any single character\".\n  // Without this, list() would silently over-match unrelated keys.\n  return s.replace(/[%_[\\]]/g, (c) => `\\\\${c}`);\n}\n\napp.http(\"getStorage\", {\n  methods: [\"GET\"],\n  authLevel: \"anonymous\", // auth is enforced by SWA in front of this + the identity check below, not by a function key\n  route: \"storage/{key}\",\n  handler: async (request, context) => {\n    const caller = requireAuth(request);\n    if (!caller) return { status: 401, jsonBody: { error: \"Not authenticated.\" } };\n\n    const rawKey = request.params.key;\n    const shared = request.query.get(\"shared\") === \"true\";\n    const resolvedKey = resolveKey(rawKey, shared, caller.identity);\n\n    try {\n      const pool = await getPool();\n      const result = await pool.request()\n        .input(\"key\", sql.NVarChar(400), resolvedKey)\n        .query(\"SELECT StorageValue FROM dbo.KeyValueStore WHERE StorageKey = @key\");\n\n      if (result.recordset.length === 0) return { status: 200, jsonBody: null };\n      return { status: 200, jsonBody: { key: rawKey, value: result.recordset[0].StorageValue, shared } };\n    } catch (err) {\n      context.error(\"getStorage failed\", err);\n      return { status: 500, jsonBody: { error: \"Could not read from storage.\" } };\n    }\n  },\n});\n\napp.http(\"setStorage\", {\n  methods: [\"PUT\"],\n  authLevel: \"anonymous\",\n  route: \"storage/{key}\",\n  handler: async (request, context) => {\n    const caller = requireAuth(request);\n    if (!caller) return { status: 401, jsonBody: { error: \"Not authenticated.\" } };\n\n    const rawKey = request.params.key;\n    const shared = request.query.get(\"shared\") === \"true\";\n    const resolvedKey = resolveKey(rawKey, shared, caller.identity);\n\n    let body;\n    try { body = await request.json(); } catch (e) { return { status: 400, jsonBody: { error: \"Request body must be JSON: { value: string }.\" } }; }\n    if (typeof body?.value !== \"string\") return { status: 400, jsonBody: { error: \"value must be a string \u2014 the app always JSON.stringifies before saving.\" } };\n\n    try {\n      const pool = await getPool();\n      await pool.request()\n        .input(\"key\", sql.NVarChar(400), resolvedKey)\n        .input(\"value\", sql.NVarChar(sql.MAX), body.value)\n        .input(\"updatedBy\", sql.NVarChar(200), caller.identity)\n        .query(`\n          MERGE dbo.KeyValueStore AS target\n          USING (SELECT @key AS StorageKey) AS src ON target.StorageKey = src.StorageKey\n          WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET StorageValue = @value, UpdatedBy = @updatedBy, UpdatedAt = SYSUTCDATETIME()\n          WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (StorageKey, StorageValue, UpdatedBy, UpdatedAt) VALUES (@key, @value, @updatedBy, SYSUTCDATETIME());\n        `);\n      return { status: 200, jsonBody: { key: rawKey, value: body.value, shared } };\n    } catch (err) {\n      context.error(\"setStorage failed\", err);\n      return { status: 500, jsonBody: { error: \"Could not write to storage.\" } };\n    }\n  },\n});\n\napp.http(\"deleteStorage\", {\n  methods: [\"DELETE\"],\n  authLevel: \"anonymous\",\n  route: \"storage/{key}\",\n  handler: async (request, context) => {\n    const caller = requireAuth(request);\n    if (!caller) return { status: 401, jsonBody: { error: \"Not authenticated.\" } };\n\n    const rawKey = request.params.key;\n    const shared = request.query.get(\"shared\") === \"true\";\n    const resolvedKey = resolveKey(rawKey, shared, caller.identity);\n\n    try {\n      const pool = await getPool();\n      await pool.request()\n        .input(\"key\", sql.NVarChar(400), resolvedKey)\n        .query(\"DELETE FROM dbo.KeyValueStore WHERE StorageKey = @key\");\n      return { status: 200, jsonBody: { key: rawKey, deleted: true, shared } };\n    } catch (err) {\n      context.error(\"deleteStorage failed\", err);\n      return { status: 500, jsonBody: { error: \"Could not delete from storage.\" } };\n    }\n  },\n});\n\napp.http(\"listStorage\", {\n  methods: [\"GET\"],\n  authLevel: \"anonymous\",\n  route: \"storage\",\n  handler: async (request, context) => {\n    const caller = requireAuth(request);\n    if (!caller) return { status: 401, jsonBody: { error: \"Not authenticated.\" } };\n\n    const prefix = request.query.get(\"prefix\") || \"\";\n    const shared = request.query.get(\"shared\") === \"true\";\n    const resolvedPrefix = shared ? prefix : `personal:${caller.identity}:${prefix}`;\n    const stripLen = shared ? 0 : `personal:${caller.identity}:`.length;\n\n    try {\n      const pool = await getPool();\n      const result = await pool.request()\n        .input(\"prefix\", sql.NVarChar(400), `${escapeLikePattern(resolvedPrefix)}%`)\n        .query(\"SELECT StorageKey FROM dbo.KeyValueStore WHERE StorageKey LIKE @prefix ESCAPE '\\\\' ORDER BY StorageKey\");\n\n      const keys = result.recordset.map((r) => r.StorageKey.slice(stripLen));\n      return { status: 200, jsonBody: { keys, prefix, shared } };\n    } catch (err) {\n      context.error(\"listStorage failed\", err);\n      return { status: 500, jsonBody: { error: \"Could not list storage keys.\" } };\n    }\n  },\n});\n";
export const PKG_SQL_SCHEMA = "/* ============================================================================\n   NESR SLK COMPETENCY FRAMEWORK SYSTEM \u2014 Azure SQL Migration Schema\n   ============================================================================\n   Target: Azure SQL Database (T-SQL). Compatible with SQL Server 2019+ on-prem\n   if Azure is not the final host.\n\n   PURPOSE\n   This schema is the Phase 2 deliverable referenced in the strategic roadmap: it\n   replaces the current single-JSON-blob storage model with a normalized relational\n   schema, so that (a) two people editing different records never collide, (b) each\n   person's client downloads only the rows relevant to them rather than the entire\n   dataset, and (c) row-level security can be enforced by the database itself rather\n   than only in client-side UI code, which today can be bypassed by anyone who opens\n   the browser console.\n\n   This file is meant to be executed top-to-bottom against a fresh Azure SQL\n   Database. It creates every table, key, index, and check constraint needed to\n   represent what the browser app currently holds in memory. It does NOT include\n   data \u2014 pair this with a one-time migration script that reads the app's\n   `Export JSON`/full-backup output and INSERTs into these tables (a Python or\n   Node script reading the exported JSON and mapping array->row is a few hours of\n   work once this schema is agreed; happy to write that once you're ready).\n\n   CONVENTIONS\n   - Every table has a surrogate BIGINT IDENTITY primary key (Id), even where a\n     natural key exists (e.g. Employees.EmployeeCode), so foreign keys stay stable\n     even if a natural key is later renamed (an element or certification name can\n     change; a foreign key referencing its Id should not have to).\n   - NVARCHAR everywhere (not VARCHAR) since the app already handles non-ASCII\n     text (Arabic transliteration, \u00b0, \u2264, \u00d7) throughout labels and names.\n   - DATETIME2 for all timestamps (higher precision than DATETIME, and the\n     modern Microsoft-recommended type).\n   - BIT for booleans, matching the app's true/false fields directly.\n   - Soft-delete is used for master data that other rows reference (Employees,\n     CatalogElements, Certifications) via an IsActive/Status flag, so historical\n     records (training, assessments, audit) are never orphaned by a delete.\n   - Audit logging happens at the DATABASE level via triggers (see the end of\n     this file), not computed client-side by diffing JSON snapshots the way the\n     current browser app does \u2014 the current approach cannot survive a browser\n     tab closing mid-edit; a trigger cannot be bypassed or missed.\n   ============================================================================ */\n\nSET ANSI_NULLS ON;\nSET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER ON;\nGO\n\n/* ============================================================================\n   1. REFERENCE / LOOKUP TABLES\n   ============================================================================ */\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.Countries (\n    Id              INT IDENTITY(1,1)      NOT NULL,\n    Name            NVARCHAR(100)          NOT NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_Countries PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_Countries_Name UNIQUE (Name)\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.Locations (\n    Id              INT IDENTITY(1,1)      NOT NULL,\n    Name            NVARCHAR(100)          NOT NULL,\n    CountryId       INT                    NULL,       -- NULL = offered under every country (matches app's \"global\" convention)\n    CONSTRAINT PK_Locations PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_Locations_Name UNIQUE (Name),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_Locations_Country FOREIGN KEY (CountryId) REFERENCES dbo.Countries(Id)\n);\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_Locations_CountryId ON dbo.Locations(CountryId);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.ProgressionGroups (\n    Id              INT IDENTITY(1,1)      NOT NULL,\n    Name            NVARCHAR(100)          NOT NULL,\n    IsExclusive     BIT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,     -- primary band vs additional (non-exclusive) group\n    Notes           NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_ProgressionGroups PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_ProgressionGroups_Name UNIQUE (Name)\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.Roles (\n    Id              INT IDENTITY(1,1)      NOT NULL,\n    Name            NVARCHAR(100)          NOT NULL,\n    CanEdit         BIT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    CanApprove      BIT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    CanAdmin        BIT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    CanManageRoles  BIT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    SubTabMode      NVARCHAR(20)           NULL,       -- e.g. 'Full' | 'Specific', matches the app's per-tab access model\n    CONSTRAINT PK_Roles PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_Roles_Name UNIQUE (Name)\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.RoleTabs (\n    RoleId          INT                    NOT NULL,\n    TabName         NVARCHAR(60)           NOT NULL,   -- 'Dashboard','Administration','Progression','Proficiency','Matrix','Tools'\n    CONSTRAINT PK_RoleTabs PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (RoleId, TabName),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_RoleTabs_Role FOREIGN KEY (RoleId) REFERENCES dbo.Roles(Id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.RoleSubTabs (\n    RoleId          INT                    NOT NULL,\n    ParentTab       NVARCHAR(60)           NOT NULL,   -- e.g. 'Administration>Catalog'\n    SubTabName      NVARCHAR(60)           NOT NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_RoleSubTabs PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (RoleId, ParentTab, SubTabName),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_RoleSubTabs_Role FOREIGN KEY (RoleId) REFERENCES dbo.Roles(Id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n);\nGO\n\n/* ============================================================================\n   2. IDENTITY \u2014 Employees and standalone (non-employee) accounts\n   ============================================================================\n   Password columns exist ONLY as a bridge during migration. Once Entra ID / SSO\n   is wired up (Phase 2 of the roadmap), PasswordHash/MustChangePassword should be\n   dropped entirely \u2014 authentication moves out of this database completely and\n   into Entra, which is both more secure and removes an entire category of risk\n   (there is no password table to ever leak). Ship this schema WITHOUT enabling\n   local password login if SSO is ready at migration time. */\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.Employees (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    EmployeeCode    NVARCHAR(30)           NOT NULL,   -- e.g. 'NESR-0002' \u2014 the app's human-facing ID\n    Name            NVARCHAR(200)          NOT NULL,\n    Email           NVARCHAR(320)          NULL,\n    CountryId       INT                    NULL,\n    LocationId      INT                    NULL,\n    Position        NVARCHAR(100)          NULL,\n    Grade           TINYINT                NULL,\n    Segment         NVARCHAR(30)           NULL,\n    HireDate        DATE                   NULL,\n    CdpStep         NVARCHAR(60)           NULL,\n    CdpStepStartAt  DATE                   NULL,\n    MentorEmployeeId BIGINT                NULL,\n    Status          NVARCHAR(20)           NOT NULL DEFAULT 'Active',   -- 'Active' | 'Inactive'\n    RoleId          INT                    NULL,\n    EntraObjectId   UNIQUEIDENTIFIER       NULL,       -- populated once SSO is live; the durable link to the Entra identity\n    PasswordHash    NVARCHAR(200)          NULL,       -- bridge only \u2014 remove once SSO replaces local auth\n    MustChangePassword BIT                 NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    PasswordChangedAt DATETIME2            NULL,\n    CreatedAt       DATETIME2              NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\n    UpdatedAt       DATETIME2              NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\n    RowVersion      ROWVERSION             NOT NULL,   -- optimistic concurrency: SQL Server maintains this automatically\n    CONSTRAINT PK_Employees PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_Employees_Code UNIQUE (EmployeeCode),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_Employees_Country FOREIGN KEY (CountryId) REFERENCES dbo.Countries(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_Employees_Location FOREIGN KEY (LocationId) REFERENCES dbo.Locations(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_Employees_Role FOREIGN KEY (RoleId) REFERENCES dbo.Roles(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_Employees_Mentor FOREIGN KEY (MentorEmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_Employees_Status CHECK (Status IN ('Active','Inactive'))\n);\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_Employees_Status ON dbo.Employees(Status) INCLUDE (Name, CountryId, LocationId);\nCREATE INDEX IX_Employees_Country ON dbo.Employees(CountryId, LocationId);\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX UX_Employees_EntraObjectId ON dbo.Employees(EntraObjectId) WHERE EntraObjectId IS NOT NULL;\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.StandaloneUsers (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    Username        NVARCHAR(60)           NOT NULL,\n    Name            NVARCHAR(200)          NULL,\n    Email           NVARCHAR(320)          NULL,\n    Position        NVARCHAR(100)          NULL,\n    RoleId          INT                    NULL,\n    EntraObjectId   UNIQUEIDENTIFIER       NULL,\n    PasswordHash    NVARCHAR(200)          NULL,       -- bridge only \u2014 see note on dbo.Employees\n    MustChangePassword BIT                 NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    PasswordChangedAt DATETIME2            NULL,\n    CreatedAt       DATETIME2              NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\n    CONSTRAINT PK_StandaloneUsers PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_StandaloneUsers_Username UNIQUE (Username),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_StandaloneUsers_Role FOREIGN KEY (RoleId) REFERENCES dbo.Roles(Id)\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.PasswordResetRequests (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    AccountType     NVARCHAR(20)           NOT NULL,   -- 'Employee' | 'Standalone'\n    EmployeeId      BIGINT                 NULL,\n    StandaloneUserId BIGINT                NULL,\n    Email           NVARCHAR(320)          NOT NULL,\n    TemporaryPasswordHash NVARCHAR(200)     NOT NULL,\n    RequestedAt     DATETIME2              NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\n    SentAt          DATETIME2              NULL,\n    SentByEmployeeId BIGINT                NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_PasswordResetRequests PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_PRR_Employee FOREIGN KEY (EmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_PRR_Standalone FOREIGN KEY (StandaloneUserId) REFERENCES dbo.StandaloneUsers(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_PRR_SentBy FOREIGN KEY (SentByEmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_PRR_AccountType CHECK (AccountType IN ('Employee','Standalone'))\n);\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_PRR_Pending ON dbo.PasswordResetRequests(SentAt) WHERE SentAt IS NULL;\nGO\n\n/* ============================================================================\n   3. COMPETENCY CATALOG \u2014 elements, groups, requirement tables\n   ============================================================================ */\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.CatalogElements (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    UnitName        NVARCHAR(150)          NOT NULL,   -- \"Tools and Services\" grouping label\n    ElementName     NVARCHAR(150)          NOT NULL,\n    MatrixCategory  NVARCHAR(60)           NULL,\n    ValidityMonths  INT                    NULL,\n    RequiredProficiencyLevel TINYINT        NULL,       -- 0-3\n    Notes           NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    IsActive        BIT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,\n    CreatedAt       DATETIME2              NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\n    CONSTRAINT PK_CatalogElements PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_CatalogElements_Name UNIQUE (ElementName),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_CatalogElements_ReqLevel CHECK (RequiredProficiencyLevel BETWEEN 0 AND 3)\n);\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_CatalogElements_Unit ON dbo.CatalogElements(UnitName) WHERE IsActive = 1;\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.ElementProgressionGroups (         -- many-to-many: an element can belong to several groups\n    ElementId       BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    ProgressionGroupId INT                 NOT NULL,\n    IsPrimary       BIT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,  -- primary band vs an additional, non-exclusive membership\n    CONSTRAINT PK_ElementProgressionGroups PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ElementId, ProgressionGroupId),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_EPG_Element FOREIGN KEY (ElementId) REFERENCES dbo.CatalogElements(Id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    CONSTRAINT FK_EPG_Group FOREIGN KEY (ProgressionGroupId) REFERENCES dbo.ProgressionGroups(Id)\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.Services (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    Name            NVARCHAR(150)          NOT NULL,\n    Notes           NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_Services PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_Services_Name UNIQUE (Name)\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.ServiceElements (                  -- which elements a service's jobs count toward\n    ServiceId       BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    ElementId       BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_ServiceElements PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ServiceId, ElementId),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_SE_Service FOREIGN KEY (ServiceId) REFERENCES dbo.Services(Id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    CONSTRAINT FK_SE_Element FOREIGN KEY (ElementId) REFERENCES dbo.CatalogElements(Id)\n);\nGO\n\n-- Requirement tables: one row per (element or reference template, level 0-3), holding\n-- the 9-cell validation criteria the app already computes proficiency against.\nCREATE TABLE dbo.RequirementTemplates (             -- the \"Reference\" library of reusable requirement sets\n    Id              INT IDENTITY(1,1)      NOT NULL,\n    Name            NVARCHAR(150)          NOT NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_RequirementTemplates PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_RequirementTemplates_Name UNIQUE (Name)\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.RequirementRows (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    -- Exactly one of TemplateId / ElementId is set, per the app's \"based on template,\n    -- with optional per-element override\" model.\n    TemplateId      INT                    NULL,\n    ElementId       BIGINT                 NULL,\n    Level           TINYINT                NOT NULL,      -- 0-3\n    InHouseRequired     BIT                NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    OnlineRequired       BIT               NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    ExposureJobsRequired INT                NULL,          -- NULL/0 = not required, matches app convention\n    ValidityYears        DECIMAL(4,1)      NULL,\n    TechRequired          BIT              NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    PracticalRequired     BIT              NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    RetainJobsPerYear     INT              NULL,\n    RetainTechRequired    BIT              NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    RetainPracticalRequired BIT            NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_RequirementRows PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_RR_Template FOREIGN KEY (TemplateId) REFERENCES dbo.RequirementTemplates(Id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    CONSTRAINT FK_RR_Element FOREIGN KEY (ElementId) REFERENCES dbo.CatalogElements(Id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    CONSTRAINT CK_RR_Level CHECK (Level BETWEEN 0 AND 3),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_RR_OneOwner CHECK ((TemplateId IS NULL) <> (ElementId IS NULL)),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_RR_TemplateLevel UNIQUE (TemplateId, Level),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_RR_ElementLevel UNIQUE (ElementId, Level)\n);\nGO\n\n/* ============================================================================\n   4. TRAINING AND CERTIFICATION CATALOGS\n   ============================================================================ */\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.TrainingCatalog (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    CourseName      NVARCHAR(200)          NOT NULL,\n    ElementId       BIGINT                 NULL,          -- NULL = certification-training or base course, per app convention\n    CertificationName NVARCHAR(150)        NULL,\n    Notes           NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_TrainingCatalog PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_TrainingCatalog_Name UNIQUE (CourseName),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_TrainingCatalog_Element FOREIGN KEY (ElementId) REFERENCES dbo.CatalogElements(Id)\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.CertificationCatalog (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    Name            NVARCHAR(150)          NOT NULL,\n    InHouseRequired  BIT                   NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    OnlineRequired    BIT                  NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    ExposureJobsRequired INT                NULL,\n    ValidityYears        DECIMAL(4,1)      NULL,\n    TechRequired          BIT              NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    PracticalRequired     BIT              NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    RetainJobsPerYear     INT              NULL,\n    RetainTechRequired    BIT              NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    RetainPracticalRequired BIT            NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    Notes           NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    IsActive        BIT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_CertificationCatalog PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_CertificationCatalog_Name UNIQUE (Name)\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.CertificationElements (            -- which elements a certification gates\n    CertificationId BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    ElementId       BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_CertificationElements PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (CertificationId, ElementId),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_CE_Cert FOREIGN KEY (CertificationId) REFERENCES dbo.CertificationCatalog(Id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    CONSTRAINT FK_CE_Element FOREIGN KEY (ElementId) REFERENCES dbo.CatalogElements(Id)\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.CertificationRoles (               -- which roles require a certification\n    CertificationId BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    RoleId          INT                    NOT NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_CertificationRoles PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (CertificationId, RoleId),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_CR_Cert FOREIGN KEY (CertificationId) REFERENCES dbo.CertificationCatalog(Id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    CONSTRAINT FK_CR_Role FOREIGN KEY (RoleId) REFERENCES dbo.Roles(Id)\n);\nGO\n\n/* ============================================================================\n   5. EMPLOYEE-INSTANCE DATA \u2014 training, experience, assessments, progress\n   ============================================================================\n   This is the highest-volume, most frequently written data in the system, and the\n   layer where row-level security matters most (an Engineer must only ever be able\n   to SELECT their own rows here \u2014 see Section 8). */\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.TrainingRecords (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    EmployeeId      BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    CourseName      NVARCHAR(200)          NOT NULL,       -- denormalized on purpose: a course rename must not rewrite history\n    ElementId       BIGINT                 NULL,\n    Delivery        NVARCHAR(60)           NULL,           -- 'In House Training' | 'OJT' | 'Online' | 'Self Training' | 'Third Party' | 'Assessment' | 'Ad hoc'\n    StartDate       DATE                   NULL,\n    EndDate         DATE                   NULL,\n    Trainer         NVARCHAR(200)          NULL,\n    Result          NVARCHAR(20)           NULL,           -- 'Pass' | 'Fail' | NULL (planned)\n    ExpiryDate      DATE                   NULL,\n    RecordsSystem   NVARCHAR(100)          NULL,\n    Notes           NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    CreatedAt       DATETIME2              NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\n    CONSTRAINT PK_TrainingRecords PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_TrainingRecords_Employee FOREIGN KEY (EmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_TrainingRecords_Element FOREIGN KEY (ElementId) REFERENCES dbo.CatalogElements(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_TrainingRecords_Result CHECK (Result IN ('Pass','Fail') OR Result IS NULL)\n);\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_TrainingRecords_Employee ON dbo.TrainingRecords(EmployeeId, ExpiryDate);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.ExperienceRecords (                -- job exposure log\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    EmployeeId      BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    ElementId       BIGINT                 NULL,\n    ServiceId       BIGINT                 NULL,\n    TicketNumber    NVARCHAR(60)           NULL,\n    WellName        NVARCHAR(150)          NULL,\n    JobDate         DATE                   NOT NULL,\n    RoleOnJob       NVARCHAR(100)          NULL,\n    Successful      NVARCHAR(10)           NULL,           -- 'Yes' | 'No'\n    ValidationStatus NVARCHAR(20)          NOT NULL DEFAULT 'Pending', -- 'Pending' | 'Approved' | 'Rejected'\n    ValidatedByEmployeeId BIGINT           NULL,\n    ApprovedAt      DATETIME2              NULL,\n    Source          NVARCHAR(30)           NOT NULL DEFAULT 'Manual entry',\n    SubmittedByEmployeeId BIGINT           NULL,\n    SubmittedAt     DATETIME2              NULL,\n    Notes           NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_ExperienceRecords PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_ExperienceRecords_Employee FOREIGN KEY (EmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_ExperienceRecords_Element FOREIGN KEY (ElementId) REFERENCES dbo.CatalogElements(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_ExperienceRecords_Service FOREIGN KEY (ServiceId) REFERENCES dbo.Services(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_ExperienceRecords_ValidatedBy FOREIGN KEY (ValidatedByEmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_ExperienceRecords_SubmittedBy FOREIGN KEY (SubmittedByEmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_ExperienceRecords_Validation CHECK (ValidationStatus IN ('Pending','Approved','Rejected')),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_ExperienceRecords_Successful CHECK (Successful IN ('Yes','No') OR Successful IS NULL)\n);\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_ExperienceRecords_Employee ON dbo.ExperienceRecords(EmployeeId, JobDate DESC);\nCREATE INDEX IX_ExperienceRecords_Pending ON dbo.ExperienceRecords(ValidationStatus) WHERE ValidationStatus = 'Pending';\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.Assessments (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    EmployeeId      BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    ElementId       BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    Level           TINYINT                NOT NULL,\n    Kind            NVARCHAR(60)           NULL,       -- 'Technical Exam' | 'Technical Interview' | 'Practical Assessment' | ...\n    Result          NVARCHAR(20)           NULL,       -- 'Pass' | 'Fail' | 'Reset' | NULL (pending)\n    Assessor        NVARCHAR(200)          NULL,\n    AssessedAt      DATE                   NULL,\n    Notes           NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    FileBlobUrl     NVARCHAR(500)          NULL,       -- pointer into Blob Storage, not the file itself\n    CONSTRAINT PK_Assessments PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_Assessments_Employee FOREIGN KEY (EmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_Assessments_Element FOREIGN KEY (ElementId) REFERENCES dbo.CatalogElements(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_Assessments_Level CHECK (Level BETWEEN 0 AND 3),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_Assessments_Result CHECK (Result IN ('Pass','Fail','Reset') OR Result IS NULL)\n);\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_Assessments_Employee ON dbo.Assessments(EmployeeId, ElementId, Level);\nCREATE INDEX IX_Assessments_Pending ON dbo.Assessments(Result) WHERE Result IS NULL;\nGO\n\n-- Progression: one row per (employee, element, level), the confirmed theory+practical\n-- state the app's proficiency engine reads. Theory/practical are split out rather than\n-- kept as nested JSON so they can be indexed and constrained individually.\nCREATE TABLE dbo.ProgressLevels (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    EmployeeId      BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    ElementId       BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    Level           TINYINT                NOT NULL,\n    TheoryMode      NVARCHAR(20)           NULL,       -- 'Exam' | 'URL' | 'File'\n    TheoryExamId    BIGINT                 NULL,\n    TheoryUrl       NVARCHAR(500)          NULL,\n    TheoryFileBlobUrl NVARCHAR(500)        NULL,\n    TheoryPass      BIT                    NULL,\n    TheoryPassAt    DATETIME2              NULL,\n    PracticalFileBlobUrl NVARCHAR(500)     NULL,\n    PracticalPass   BIT                    NULL,\n    PracticalPassAt DATETIME2              NULL,\n    ConfirmedAt     DATETIME2              NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_ProgressLevels PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_ProgressLevels UNIQUE (EmployeeId, ElementId, Level),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_ProgressLevels_Employee FOREIGN KEY (EmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_ProgressLevels_Element FOREIGN KEY (ElementId) REFERENCES dbo.CatalogElements(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_ProgressLevels_Level CHECK (Level BETWEEN 0 AND 3)\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.CertificationProgress (            -- mirrors ProgressLevels, for certification tasks\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    EmployeeId      BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    CertificationId BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    TheoryPass      BIT                    NULL,\n    TheoryPassAt    DATETIME2              NULL,\n    PracticalPass   BIT                    NULL,\n    PracticalPassAt DATETIME2              NULL,\n    ConfirmedAt     DATETIME2              NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_CertificationProgress PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT UQ_CertificationProgress UNIQUE (EmployeeId, CertificationId),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_CertProgress_Employee FOREIGN KEY (EmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_CertProgress_Cert FOREIGN KEY (CertificationId) REFERENCES dbo.CertificationCatalog(Id)\n);\nGO\n\n-- Legacy manually-tracked certification records (kept separate from the computed\n-- CertificationProgress model, matching the app's own \"state.certs\" vs \"cert\n-- programme\" distinction).\nCREATE TABLE dbo.LegacyCertificationRecords (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    EmployeeId      BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    CertificationName NVARCHAR(150)        NOT NULL,\n    IssueDate       DATE                   NULL,\n    ExpiryDate      DATE                   NULL,\n    ValidatedBy     NVARCHAR(200)          NULL,\n    RecordsSystem   NVARCHAR(100)          NULL,\n    Notes           NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_LegacyCertificationRecords PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_LegacyCert_Employee FOREIGN KEY (EmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id)\n);\nGO\n\n/* ============================================================================\n   6. ONLINE EXAMS\n   ============================================================================ */\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.Exams (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    Title           NVARCHAR(200)          NOT NULL,\n    Description     NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    PassPercentage  TINYINT                NOT NULL DEFAULT 70,\n    Source          NVARCHAR(200)          NULL,\n    TargetType      NVARCHAR(20)           NOT NULL DEFAULT 'Element',   -- 'Element' | 'Certification'\n    ElementId       BIGINT                 NULL,\n    CertificationId BIGINT                 NULL,\n    MaxAttempts     TINYINT                NOT NULL DEFAULT 3,\n    WaitHoursBetweenAttempts INT           NOT NULL DEFAULT 24,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_Exams PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_Exams_Element FOREIGN KEY (ElementId) REFERENCES dbo.CatalogElements(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_Exams_Cert FOREIGN KEY (CertificationId) REFERENCES dbo.CertificationCatalog(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_Exams_TargetType CHECK (TargetType IN ('Element','Certification'))\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.ExamQuestions (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    ExamId          BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    QuestionText    NVARCHAR(MAX)          NOT NULL,\n    IsMultiSelect   BIT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    Points          TINYINT                NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,\n    ImageBlobUrl    NVARCHAR(500)          NULL,\n    DisplayOrder    INT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_ExamQuestions PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_ExamQuestions_Exam FOREIGN KEY (ExamId) REFERENCES dbo.Exams(Id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.ExamQuestionOptions (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    QuestionId      BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    OptionText      NVARCHAR(MAX)          NOT NULL,\n    IsCorrect       BIT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    DisplayOrder    INT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_ExamQuestionOptions PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_EQO_Question FOREIGN KEY (QuestionId) REFERENCES dbo.ExamQuestions(Id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n);\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.ExamResults (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    ExamId          BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    EmployeeId      BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    AttemptedAt     DATETIME2              NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\n    ScorePercent    DECIMAL(5,2)           NOT NULL,\n    Passed          BIT                    NOT NULL,\n    AnswersJson     NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,       -- raw answer payload for audit/dispute review; not queried directly\n    CONSTRAINT PK_ExamResults PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_ExamResults_Exam FOREIGN KEY (ExamId) REFERENCES dbo.Exams(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_ExamResults_Employee FOREIGN KEY (EmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_ExamResults_AnswersJson CHECK (AnswersJson IS NULL OR ISJSON(AnswersJson) = 1)\n);\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_ExamResults_Employee ON dbo.ExamResults(EmployeeId, ExamId, AttemptedAt DESC);\nGO\n\n/* ============================================================================\n   7. AUDIT TRAIL \u2014 database-enforced, not client-computed\n   ============================================================================\n   The current browser app diffs JSON snapshots on every autosave to build an\n   audit trail, which cannot survive a closed tab and can theoretically be bypassed\n   by anyone editing the stored data directly. A database trigger is authoritative:\n   it fires on every UPDATE regardless of which application, script, or person made\n   the change, and cannot be skipped by the client. */\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.AuditLog (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    ChangedAt       DATETIME2              NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\n    ChangedByEmployeeId BIGINT             NULL,       -- resolved from SESSION_CONTEXT, set by the API layer per request\n    TableName       NVARCHAR(128)          NOT NULL,\n    RecordId        BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    ColumnName      NVARCHAR(128)          NULL,\n    OldValue        NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    NewValue        NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    Action          NVARCHAR(10)           NOT NULL,   -- 'INSERT' | 'UPDATE' | 'DELETE'\n    CONSTRAINT PK_AuditLog PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_AuditLog_Action CHECK (Action IN ('INSERT','UPDATE','DELETE'))\n);\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_AuditLog_Record ON dbo.AuditLog(TableName, RecordId, ChangedAt DESC);\nGO\n\n/* Example trigger \u2014 apply the same pattern to every table that needs an audit trail\n   (Assessments, ExperienceRecords, ProgressLevels, Employees, CertificationProgress\n   are the highest-value candidates, matching what the current app's client-side\n   audit trail already tracks). ChangedByEmployeeId is read from SESSION_CONTEXT,\n   which the API layer sets once per authenticated request \u2014 this is what makes the\n   audit trail trustworthy: the API sets the identity, not the client. */\nCREATE TRIGGER trg_Assessments_Audit\nON dbo.Assessments\nAFTER UPDATE\nAS\nBEGIN\n    SET NOCOUNT ON;\n    INSERT INTO dbo.AuditLog (ChangedByEmployeeId, TableName, RecordId, ColumnName, OldValue, NewValue, Action)\n    SELECT\n        CAST(SESSION_CONTEXT(N'EmployeeId') AS BIGINT),\n        'Assessments', i.Id, 'Result', CAST(d.Result AS NVARCHAR(MAX)), CAST(i.Result AS NVARCHAR(MAX)), 'UPDATE'\n    FROM inserted i\n    JOIN deleted d ON d.Id = i.Id\n    WHERE ISNULL(d.Result,'') <> ISNULL(i.Result,'');\n    -- Repeat the SELECT ... WHERE pattern per column of interest, or use a generic\n    -- column-diffing procedure if the audit surface grows; keeping it explicit here\n    -- because explicit is easier to review in a compliance context than clever.\nEND;\nGO\n\n/* ============================================================================\n   8. ROW-LEVEL SECURITY \u2014 enforced by the database, not just the client UI\n   ============================================================================\n   Today, an Engineer's UI hides other employees' records, but the browser already\n   has the FULL dataset in memory \u2014 anyone can read it via the browser console.\n   Row-level security makes that structurally impossible: the database itself never\n   returns rows the calling identity is not allowed to see, regardless of what the\n   client requests. This requires the API layer to set SESSION_CONTEXT once per\n   authenticated request (immediately after validating the caller's Entra token). */\n\nCREATE FUNCTION dbo.fn_CanSeeEmployee(@TargetEmployeeId BIGINT)\nRETURNS TABLE\nWITH SCHEMABINDING\nAS\nRETURN\n    SELECT 1 AS fn_result\n    WHERE\n        -- Admins and Approvers see everyone.\n        CAST(SESSION_CONTEXT(N'IsAdminOrApprover') AS BIT) = 1\n        -- Everyone else sees only their own record.\n        OR @TargetEmployeeId = CAST(SESSION_CONTEXT(N'EmployeeId') AS BIGINT);\nGO\n\nCREATE SECURITY POLICY dbo.EmployeeScopePolicy\n    ADD FILTER PREDICATE dbo.fn_CanSeeEmployee(EmployeeId) ON dbo.TrainingRecords,\n    ADD FILTER PREDICATE dbo.fn_CanSeeEmployee(EmployeeId) ON dbo.ExperienceRecords,\n    ADD FILTER PREDICATE dbo.fn_CanSeeEmployee(EmployeeId) ON dbo.Assessments,\n    ADD FILTER PREDICATE dbo.fn_CanSeeEmployee(EmployeeId) ON dbo.ProgressLevels,\n    ADD FILTER PREDICATE dbo.fn_CanSeeEmployee(EmployeeId) ON dbo.CertificationProgress,\n    ADD FILTER PREDICATE dbo.fn_CanSeeEmployee(EmployeeId) ON dbo.ExamResults\n    WITH (STATE = ON);\nGO\n\n/* ============================================================================\n   9. BACKUP / SNAPSHOT METADATA\n   ============================================================================\n   Azure SQL's own Point-in-Time Restore (35-day rolling window by default,\n   configurable) replaces the app's current manual 12-hour backup mechanism\n   entirely \u2014 this table is only needed if you want an in-app \"named snapshot\"\n   feature (e.g. before a bulk import) layered on top of PITR, not to replicate\n   what Azure already gives you for free. */\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.NamedSnapshots (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    Label           NVARCHAR(200)          NOT NULL,\n    CreatedAt       DATETIME2              NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\n    CreatedByEmployeeId BIGINT             NULL,\n    RestorePointUtc DATETIME2              NOT NULL,   -- the timestamp to pass to Azure SQL's PITR restore command\n    CONSTRAINT PK_NamedSnapshots PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_NamedSnapshots_By FOREIGN KEY (CreatedByEmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id)\n);\nGO\n\n/* ============================================================================\n   END OF SCHEMA\n   ============================================================================\n   NOT included here, deliberately: the migration script that reads the app's\n   `Export JSON` / full-backup output and populates these tables \u2014 that is a\n   short, mechanical script best written against a REAL export from the live\n   system rather than the seed data, so field-mapping edge cases surface once,\n   against real data, rather than being guessed at twice.\n   ============================================================================ */\n\n/* ============================================================================\n   10. INDEX REVIEW \u2014 added against the app's actual query patterns\n   ============================================================================\n   The indexes above were designed around each table's own primary access path\n   (an employee's own training, an employee's own jobs). Reviewing them against\n   how the app's screens actually query the data surfaces a specific, recurring\n   gap: several of the busiest screens query the OPPOSITE direction \u2014 not \"give\n   me this one employee's records\" but \"give me every employee's status for\n   this one element/certification/course\" \u2014 because that is literally what a\n   matrix view is. Composite keys and indexes that lead with EmployeeId do not\n   serve that access path efficiently; SQL Server would have to scan every\n   employee's rows to answer a column-oriented question. Each addition below\n   is tied to a specific screen, not added speculatively. */\n\n-- Matrix -> Proficiency tab: for a filtered set of elements, every active\n-- employee's level on each. Today's UQ_ProgressLevels(EmployeeId, ElementId,\n-- Level) makes this a full-table scan; this index makes it an index seek per\n-- element, which is what the screen actually asks for.\nCREATE INDEX IX_ProgressLevels_ByElement\n    ON dbo.ProgressLevels(ElementId, Level)\n    INCLUDE (EmployeeId, ConfirmedAt, TheoryPassAt, PracticalPassAt);\nGO\n\n-- Matrix -> Certifications tab: same reasoning, for the certification programme.\nCREATE INDEX IX_CertificationProgress_ByCert\n    ON dbo.CertificationProgress(CertificationId)\n    INCLUDE (EmployeeId, ConfirmedAt, TheoryPassAt, PracticalPassAt);\nGO\n\n-- Retention engine: \"how many approved, successful jobs has ANY employee logged\n-- against element X in the last 12 months\" is computed for the whole team at\n-- once whenever Matrix or Dashboard renders \u2014 an element-first, date-filtered\n-- scan. IX_ExperienceRecords_Employee (added earlier) serves the Proficiency\n-- report's opposite question; this serves the retention/matrix computation.\nCREATE INDEX IX_ExperienceRecords_ByElement\n    ON dbo.ExperienceRecords(ElementId, ValidationStatus, SuccessFlag, JobDate)\n    INCLUDE (EmployeeId)\n    WHERE ValidationStatus = 'Approved' AND SuccessFlag = 1;\nGO\n\n-- Certification/role scoping: certsForRole-style checks (used by the\n-- notification centre, Risk Tool's lockout, and Proficiency's required-\n-- certifications block) filter active employees by RoleId repeatedly.\n-- IX_Employees_Status already covers Status; this adds RoleId so the\n-- optimizer can seek directly rather than filtering a Status scan by role\n-- after the fact.\nCREATE INDEX IX_Employees_Role\n    ON dbo.Employees(RoleId)\n    INCLUDE (Name, Status)\n    WHERE Status = 'Active';\nGO\n\n-- Audit trail, default view: \"show recent changes across everything\" (no\n-- entity or record filter yet \u2014 the state the tab opens in) needs a\n-- chronological scan. IX_AuditLog_Record (added earlier) serves \"show this\n-- one record's history\"; this serves the tab's landing view, and the \"who\n-- changed things\" filter the UI also offers.\nCREATE INDEX IX_AuditLog_ChangedAt\n    ON dbo.AuditLog(ChangedAt DESC)\n    INCLUDE (TableName, RecordId, ChangedBy, FieldName);\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_AuditLog_ChangedBy\n    ON dbo.AuditLog(ChangedBy, ChangedAt DESC);\nGO\n\n/* ---- Considered and deliberately NOT added ----\n   Recording these so a future reviewer does not re-propose them without\n   re-checking the same reasoning.\n\n   - A persisted computed \"IsExpired\" column with its own index on tables\n     like TrainingRecords/CertificationProgress. SQL Server seeks efficiently\n     on \"ExpiryDate < @Today\" against a plain index on the date column itself;\n     a computed flag would need to be kept in sync by a trigger for no real\n     seek-performance gain, adding write-path complexity for nothing.\n   - Indexes on the small reference/junction tables (Roles, RoleTabs,\n     CertificationElements, CertificationRoles, ServiceElements,\n     ElementProgressionGroups, Countries, Locations). These hold at most a\n     few hundred rows across the whole system; a full scan of the clustered\n     PK is already faster than the overhead of maintaining a secondary index,\n     and the query optimizer will choose exactly that.\n   - Indexing ExamQuestions/ExamQuestionOptions beyond their PKs. Read\n     entirely as \"every question for exam X\" (a handful of rows) whenever\n     someone takes that exam \u2014 no filtering pattern exists that a secondary\n     index would speed up. */\n\n/* ---- Ongoing index health: a \"meta\" review, not a one-time task ----\n   Indexes chosen against today's known query patterns will drift out of date\n   as the app grows screens the schema's designers didn't anticipate. Run this\n   periodically (quarterly is reasonable) rather than treating the list above\n   as final \u2014 it surfaces both indexes that are not paying for their\n   maintenance cost and reads that are still doing full scans despite an\n   index existing. */\n\n-- Indexes that exist but are rarely or never used for reads relative to how\n-- often they are written to \u2014 candidates for removal, since every index\n-- slows down every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on its table.\nSELECT\n    OBJECT_NAME(s.object_id)   AS TableName,\n    i.name                     AS IndexName,\n    s.user_seeks, s.user_scans, s.user_lookups,\n    s.user_updates,\n    CASE WHEN (s.user_seeks + s.user_scans + s.user_lookups) = 0 THEN 'UNUSED \u2014 review for removal'\n         WHEN s.user_updates > (s.user_seeks + s.user_scans + s.user_lookups) * 10 THEN 'WRITE-HEAVY vs reads \u2014 review cost/benefit'\n         ELSE 'OK' END        AS Assessment\nFROM sys.dm_db_index_usage_stats s\nJOIN sys.indexes i ON i.object_id = s.object_id AND i.index_id = s.index_id\nWHERE OBJECTPROPERTY(s.object_id, 'IsUserTable') = 1 AND i.name IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY (s.user_seeks + s.user_scans + s.user_lookups) ASC;\nGO\n\n-- Queries doing large scans without a supporting index (the mirror-image\n-- check) \u2014 surfaces missing indexes the app's real usage reveals over time\n-- that this document's authors could not have anticipated in advance.\nSELECT TOP 20\n    d.avg_user_impact, d.avg_total_user_cost, s.user_seeks + s.user_scans AS TimesUsed,\n    'CREATE INDEX IX_' + OBJECT_NAME(d.object_id) + '_' + CONVERT(VARCHAR, d.index_handle)\n        + ' ON ' + d.statement + ' (' + ISNULL(d.equality_columns, '') + ISNULL(d.inequality_columns, '') + ')'\n        + ISNULL(' INCLUDE (' + d.included_columns + ')', '') AS SuggestedIndex\nFROM sys.dm_db_missing_index_details d\nJOIN sys.dm_db_missing_index_groups g ON d.index_handle = g.index_handle\nJOIN sys.dm_db_missing_index_group_stats s ON g.index_group_handle = s.group_handle\nORDER BY d.avg_user_impact DESC;\nGO\n\n/* ============================================================================\n   11. CACHING SUPPORT \u2014 a materialized cache table for the app's one genuinely\n       expensive computation\n   ============================================================================\n   The app's client-side engine (useDerived) computes, on every render, for\n   every employee x every element: the effective proficiency level, whether it\n   has expired, the retention status (jobs logged in the last 12 months vs the\n   requirement), and the same again for every certification x employee via\n   computeCertStatus. That is O(employees x elements x experience records) \u2014\n   cheap enough client-side for one person's browser with today's headcount,\n   expensive as a per-request server computation once many people load\n   Dashboard or Matrix at once, and it recomputes results that usually have not\n   changed since the last request.\n\n   The fix is not \"add more indexes to the raw tables\" \u2014 section 10 already\n   covers that for queries that DO need to hit them directly. The fix is to\n   stop recomputing the derived result on every read at all: materialize it\n   into its own table, keep it correct with background jobs (section 12), and\n   let every read-heavy screen (Dashboard, Matrix, Proficiency, the\n   notification centre, Risk Tool's lockout check) query this table directly\n   instead of re-deriving the answer from raw rows every time. */\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.ComputedProficiency (\n    EmployeeId      BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    ElementId       BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    EffectiveLevel  TINYINT                NOT NULL,   -- 0-3, after applying expiry\n    RecordedLevel   TINYINT                NOT NULL,   -- the level actually confirmed, before expiry is applied\n    IsExpired       BIT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    AchievedAt      DATETIME2              NULL,\n    ValidUntil      DATETIME2              NULL,       -- NULL = no assessment date recorded (see app's \"undated\" handling)\n    JobsLast12Months INT                   NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    RequiredJobsPerYear INT                NULL,\n    RetentionStatus NVARCHAR(40)           NOT NULL,   -- 'On track' | 'AT RISK' | 'EXPIRED' | 'Exposure only' | 'No assessment date'\n    ComputedAt      DATETIME2              NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\n    CONSTRAINT PK_ComputedProficiency PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (EmployeeId, ElementId),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_ComputedProficiency_Employee FOREIGN KEY (EmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_ComputedProficiency_Element FOREIGN KEY (ElementId) REFERENCES dbo.CatalogElements(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_ComputedProficiency_Level CHECK (EffectiveLevel BETWEEN 0 AND 3 AND RecordedLevel BETWEEN 0 AND 3)\n);\nGO\n-- Serves the Matrix -> Proficiency column-oriented read directly, with the cache\n-- already in the shape the screen wants \u2014 no join back to ProgressLevels needed\n-- for the common case.\nCREATE INDEX IX_ComputedProficiency_ByElement\n    ON dbo.ComputedProficiency(ElementId, EffectiveLevel)\n    INCLUDE (EmployeeId, IsExpired, RetentionStatus);\nGO\n-- Serves the notification centre and Dashboard's \"at risk\" / \"expired\" counts.\nCREATE INDEX IX_ComputedProficiency_Retention\n    ON dbo.ComputedProficiency(RetentionStatus)\n    WHERE RetentionStatus IN ('AT RISK', 'EXPIRED');\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.ComputedCertificationStatus (\n    EmployeeId      BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    CertificationId BIGINT                 NOT NULL,\n    StatusCode      TINYINT                NOT NULL,   -- 0 not done/expired, 1 in progress, 2 expiring soon, 3 valid\n    AllTasksDone    BIT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    CompletedAt     DATETIME2              NULL,\n    ValidUntil      DATETIME2              NULL,\n    ComputedAt      DATETIME2              NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\n    CONSTRAINT PK_ComputedCertStatus PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (EmployeeId, CertificationId),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_ComputedCertStatus_Employee FOREIGN KEY (EmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_ComputedCertStatus_Cert FOREIGN KEY (CertificationId) REFERENCES dbo.CertificationCatalog(Id),\n    CONSTRAINT CK_ComputedCertStatus_Code CHECK (StatusCode BETWEEN 0 AND 3)\n);\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_ComputedCertStatus_ByCert\n    ON dbo.ComputedCertificationStatus(CertificationId, StatusCode)\n    INCLUDE (EmployeeId, ValidUntil);\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_ComputedCertStatus_Lockout\n    ON dbo.ComputedCertificationStatus(EmployeeId)\n    INCLUDE (CertificationId, StatusCode)\n    WHERE StatusCode = 0;      -- serves Risk Tool's lockout check directly: \"which of this\n                                -- employee's required certifications are not-done/expired\"\nGO\n\n/* ---- Cache tiers, and what belongs in each ----\n   Not every kind of data should be cached the same way. Three tiers, in order\n   of how often they change:\n\n   1. RARELY CHANGES \u2014 reference/catalog data (CatalogElements, CertificationCatalog,\n      Roles, Countries, Locations, ProgressionGroups, RequirementRows). Cache in\n      Azure Cache for Redis with a long TTL (hours) or no TTL at all, invalidated\n      explicitly by the write path (an admin editing the catalog fires a targeted\n      cache-clear, not a wait-for-expiry). This is a classic cache-aside pattern\n      and needs nothing beyond Redis itself.\n\n   2. CHANGES ON RECORD EVENTS, EXPENSIVE TO DERIVE \u2014 exactly what\n      ComputedProficiency and ComputedCertificationStatus exist for. This is NOT a\n      Redis cache; it is a real table, because the read pattern needs to filter,\n      sort, and join (Matrix's per-element breakdown, Dashboard's aggregate\n      counts) \u2014 a key-value cache cannot serve that. Redis sits ON TOP of these\n      tables for the hottest specific queries (e.g. \"today's Dashboard KPI\n      payload\") with a short TTL (1-5 minutes), but the table itself is the\n      source of truth for anything that needs to be queried rather than fetched\n      by key.\n\n   3. NEVER CACHED \u2014 anything inside an active edit (a person mid-way through\n      filling in an assessment form, a manual job entry in progress). Caching\n      partial/unsaved state anywhere the browser cannot own it risks showing a\n      stale value to a second person as if it were current. */\n\n/* ============================================================================\n   12. BACKGROUND TASK INFRASTRUCTURE \u2014 what runs off the request path, and how\n   ============================================================================\n   Two different triggering models cover everything the app needs to do\n   asynchronously, and the distinction matters for which Azure primitive to use:\n\n   - TIMER-TRIGGERED: work that needs to happen periodically regardless of\n     whether anything changed (a certification simply reaching its expiry date\n     with no new data written is still a state change the system must notice).\n   - EVENT/QUEUE-TRIGGERED: work that should happen promptly after a specific\n     write, without making the person who made that write wait for it.\n\n   Both are ordinary Azure Functions triggers (TimerTrigger and QueueTrigger\n   respectively) writing against the tables below \u2014 no Durable Functions\n   orchestration is needed for any of these; each task is a single unit of\n   work, not a multi-step workflow, except the one-time data migration noted\n   in section 2 of this file's header, which is exactly the kind of multi-step,\n   resumable job Durable Functions exists for and nothing else here is. */\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.BackgroundJobQueue (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    JobType         NVARCHAR(60)           NOT NULL,   -- see the catalogue below\n    PayloadJson     NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,        -- e.g. {\"EmployeeId\": 123} for a targeted recompute\n    Status          NVARCHAR(20)           NOT NULL DEFAULT 'Pending',  -- Pending | Processing | Done | Failed\n    Attempts        INT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    LastError       NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    CreatedAt       DATETIME2              NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\n    ProcessedAt     DATETIME2              NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_BackgroundJobQueue PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id)\n);\nGO\n-- Serves the queue-trigger's own \"what's still pending\" scan.\nCREATE INDEX IX_BackgroundJobQueue_Pending\n    ON dbo.BackgroundJobQueue(Status, CreatedAt)\n    WHERE Status IN ('Pending', 'Processing');\nGO\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.NotificationDispatchQueue (\n    Id              BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)   NOT NULL,\n    Channel         NVARCHAR(20)           NOT NULL,   -- 'Email' | 'Push'\n    RecipientEmployeeId BIGINT             NULL,\n    RecipientAddress NVARCHAR(320)         NULL,        -- resolved email/push target, for standalone accounts too\n    Subject         NVARCHAR(300)          NULL,\n    Body            NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    Status          NVARCHAR(20)           NOT NULL DEFAULT 'Pending',  -- Pending | Sent | Failed\n    Attempts        INT                    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    CreatedAt       DATETIME2              NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\n    SentAt          DATETIME2              NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT PK_NotificationDispatchQueue PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (Id),\n    CONSTRAINT FK_NotifQueue_Employee FOREIGN KEY (RecipientEmployeeId) REFERENCES dbo.Employees(Id)\n);\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_NotificationDispatchQueue_Pending\n    ON dbo.NotificationDispatchQueue(Status, CreatedAt)\n    WHERE Status = 'Pending';\nGO\n\n/* ---- Background job catalogue \u2014 what runs, on which trigger, and why ----\n\n   TIMER-TRIGGERED (Azure Functions TimerTrigger):\n\n   - RecomputeAllProficiency (nightly, e.g. 02:00 local)\n     Full recompute of ComputedProficiency and ComputedCertificationStatus for\n     every active employee. Catches the case the app's own client-side engine\n     already has to handle specially: a certification or level can expire\n     purely because time passed, with no row having been written that day.\n     Without this sweep, ComputedProficiency would only ever be as fresh as\n     the last event-triggered recompute, and a person whose certification\n     quietly lapsed over a weekend would still show as compliant on Monday\n     morning until someone happened to touch their record.\n\n   - SweepExpiryNotifications (daily, after RecomputeAllProficiency completes)\n     Scans ComputedProficiency and ComputedCertificationStatus for anything\n     newly AT RISK, EXPIRED, or expiring inside 30 days, and writes rows into\n     NotificationDispatchQueue. Runs after the recompute specifically so it\n     sees today's true state, not yesterday's.\n\n   - CreateNamedSnapshot (every 12 hours, matching the app's current manual\n     backup cadence, until Azure SQL's own Point-in-Time Restore is trusted as\n     the sole backup mechanism per section 9's note)\n     Writes a row to dbo.NamedSnapshots recording the PITR restore point.\n\n   - IndexHealthReview (weekly)\n     Runs the two DMV queries at the end of section 10 and emails the results\n     to whoever owns the database, rather than relying on someone remembering\n     to run them by hand quarterly.\n\n   EVENT/QUEUE-TRIGGERED (Azure Functions QueueTrigger, fed by\n   dbo.BackgroundJobQueue):\n\n   - RecomputeProficiencyForEmployee({EmployeeId})\n     Enqueued by the API layer immediately after an INSERT/UPDATE to\n     TrainingRecords, ExperienceRecords, Assessments, or ProgressLevels for\n     that employee. A single-employee recompute is cheap (a few dozen rows at\n     most), so this keeps ComputedProficiency correct within seconds of a\n     write rather than waiting for the nightly sweep \u2014 the nightly job exists\n     for time-based drift, this one exists for data-based drift.\n\n   - RecomputeCertStatusForEmployee({EmployeeId})\n     Same pattern, enqueued after a write to CertificationProgress or\n     LegacyCertificationRecords.\n\n   - DispatchNotification({Id}) reading dbo.NotificationDispatchQueue\n     Actually sends the email (Microsoft Graph sendMail) or push (SignalR).\n     Decoupled from whatever enqueued it specifically so a slow or failed\n     Graph API call never makes the triggering user-facing request wait on it\n     \u2014 the person approving a job or confirming a level should never notice\n     that a notification was also queued behind the scenes.\n\n   - InvalidateCatalogCache({EntityType, Id})\n     Enqueued after any write to a Tier-1 reference table (see the cache-tier\n     note above). Clears the specific Redis key rather than the whole cache,\n     so one admin editing one certification does not force every other\n     concurrent user's catalog cache to cold-start.\n\n   Each queue-triggered function reads its own job row, sets Status =\n   'Processing', does the work, and sets Status = 'Done' or 'Failed' with\n   LastError populated \u2014 Attempts increments on every pickup, and a function\n   should stop retrying and alert a human past a small fixed limit (3 is\n   reasonable) rather than retry forever against a job that will never\n   succeed. */\nGO\n\n/* ============================================================================\n   13. KEY-VALUE TIER \u2014 a deliberate, named stepping stone before the full\n       normalized model above is actually used\n   ============================================================================\n   Everything in sections 1-12 is the target end state: a normalized schema\n   with row-level security enforced by the database, per-entity endpoints,\n   and a real cache/background-job layer sitting in front of it. Getting\n   there also means rewriting how the browser app fetches data \u2014 today it\n   reads and writes one big JSON document per screen load, not individual\n   rows, and every component would need to change to speak the normalized\n   model instead.\n\n   That is real, separate work. It should not block the smaller, achievable\n   step of getting the team off browser-local storage and onto a real shared\n   Azure SQL database with real Entra authentication \u2014 which is what \"ready\n   for multi-user sharing\" concretely requires today. This table exists for\n   exactly that: the browser app's existing get/set/delete/list calls (see\n   src/storagePolyfill.js in the standalone package) point at this table\n   instead of IndexedDB, with zero changes to how App.jsx reads or writes\n   its data. Multi-user sharing becomes real immediately. Row-level security\n   does NOT \u2014 a value in this table is an opaque JSON blob to the database,\n   which cannot filter inside it. That gap closes only when the normalized\n   schema above is actually adopted as the storage model, not before. */\n\nCREATE TABLE dbo.KeyValueStore (\n    StorageKey      NVARCHAR(400)          NOT NULL,\n    StorageValue    NVARCHAR(MAX)          NULL,\n    UpdatedBy       NVARCHAR(200)          NULL,       -- the caller's verified Entra identity, set server-side \u2014 never trust a client-supplied \"who saved this\"\n    UpdatedAt       DATETIME2              NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\n    CONSTRAINT PK_KeyValueStore PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (StorageKey)\n);\nGO\n-- Serves the \"list by prefix\" call (backups, per-employee exam images, and\n-- similar key families the app already groups by a shared prefix).\nCREATE INDEX IX_KeyValueStore_Prefix ON dbo.KeyValueStore(StorageKey) INCLUDE (UpdatedAt);\nGO\n";
export const PKG_TOP_README = "# NESR SLK Competency Framework System \u2014 deployment package\n\nGenerated from the live running app on {DATE} by {USER}.\n\n## Before you do anything else: add App.jsx\n\nThis package intentionally does NOT include `nesr-wl-competency-app/src/App.jsx`.\nA running web app cannot reliably read back its own original source code \u2014\nthere is no browser API for a page to introspect the exact source it was\nbuilt from, especially once bundled or transformed for display. Rather than\nattempt something fragile that could silently ship a stale or broken copy,\nthis package leaves that one file out on purpose.\n\n**Copy the App.jsx you already have** \u2014 the file downloadable throughout\nthis conversation as \"NESR SLK Competency System App\" \u2014 into:\n\n    nesr-wl-competency-app/src/App.jsx\n\nA minimal placeholder is there in the meantime so `npm install && npm run\nbuild` succeeds even if you run it before doing this \u2014 it will show a plain\non-screen message telling you the same thing, rather than fail outright.\n\n## If you hit a build error before adding App.jsx\n\nThe placeholder is deliberately real, working React \u2014 if `npm run build`\nfails on a fresh unzip with the placeholder still in place, the problem is\nin this package's own scaffolding, not in your App.jsx. Two things worth\nchecking first, since they're the most common causes of \"file not found\" /\nmodule resolution errors in a Node project:\n\n1. **Node version.** This package is tested against Node 20 \u2014 see\n   `nesr-wl-competency-app/.nvmrc`. A much older or newer Node can resolve\n   dependencies differently. `nvm use` if you have nvm installed.\n2. **A clean install.** Delete `node_modules` and any lockfile if you've\n   run `npm install` more than once or interrupted it partway:\n   `rm -rf node_modules package-lock.json && npm install`\n\nEvery dependency version in this package is pinned exactly (no `^` ranges)\nspecifically so what installs today matches what this was tested against \u2014\nif you still hit a resolution error after both of the above, please share\nthe exact error text; that's the fastest way to pin down what's different\nabout your environment.\n\n## What's in here\n\n- `nesr-wl-competency-app/` \u2014 the frontend. `npm install && npm run dev` for\n  standalone mode; see its own README for connecting to the real API.\n- `nesr-wl-api/` \u2014 the Azure Functions backend. See its README for the full\n  deployment sequence (SQL, Managed Identity, Entra auth, Static Web Apps).\n- `docs/NESR_WL_Migration_Schema.sql` \u2014 the complete database schema. Run\n  this FIRST against your Azure SQL Database.\n- `docs/NESR_WL_Data_Import.sql` \u2014 run this SECOND, after the schema. It\n  contains the actual data that was live in the app when this package was\n  generated \u2014 every employee, every record, every setting \u2014 as MERGE\n  statements against dbo.KeyValueStore (the key-value tier the API actually\n  implements today; see the roadmap for the separate, larger migration to\n  the fully normalized tables).\n\n## Order of operations\n\n1. `sqlcmd ... -i docs/NESR_WL_Migration_Schema.sql`\n2. `sqlcmd ... -i docs/NESR_WL_Data_Import.sql`\n3. Follow `nesr-wl-api/README.md` to deploy the API\n4. Copy your App.jsx into `nesr-wl-competency-app/src/App.jsx`\n5. Follow `nesr-wl-competency-app/README.md` to deploy the frontend\n\nEvery command is in the two component READMEs \u2014 this file is just the map.\n";
export const PKG_APP_PLACEHOLDER = "import React from \"react\";\n\n// Placeholder \u2014 replace this file with the App.jsx you already have from\n// the conversation this package was generated from (downloadable there as\n// \"NESR SLK Competency System App\"). See ../../README.md for why this ships\n// separately rather than being included automatically.\n//\n// This file is deliberately a real, valid component \u2014 not just a comment \u2014\n// so `npm install && npm run build` succeeds even before you've done the\n// swap, and shows this message instead of failing with a build error.\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <div style={{\n      minHeight: \"100vh\", display: \"flex\", alignItems: \"center\", justifyContent: \"center\",\n      fontFamily: \"system-ui, sans-serif\", background: \"#EEF1EC\", color: \"#1B2420\", padding: 24,\n    }}>\n      <div style={{ maxWidth: 480, textAlign: \"center\" }}>\n        <div style={{ fontSize: 22, fontWeight: 700, marginBottom: 12 }}>\n          Add App.jsx to see the real application\n        </div>\n        <div style={{ fontSize: 14, lineHeight: 1.6, color: \"#5E6B62\" }}>\n          This is a placeholder. Replace <code>src/App.jsx</code> with the file you\n          already have from this project's conversation \u2014 see this package's top-level\n          README.md for the exact one-step instruction.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n";
export const PKG_TEST_MAIN_JSX = "// Standalone test package \u2014 always uses the IndexedDB polyfill, no API,\n// no backend, no configuration needed. See storagePolyfill.js for exactly\n// what that means and its limitations (single-browser only, not multi-user).\nimport \"./storagePolyfill.js\";\n\nimport React from \"react\";\nimport ReactDOM from \"react-dom/client\";\nimport App from \"./App.jsx\";\n\n// No <React.StrictMode> wrapper \u2014 see the deployment package's main.jsx for\n// why: this app's timers/intervals were built and tested only against\n// Claude's artifact environment, which does not run StrictMode, and a\n// dev-mode double-invoke risks a duplicated backup snapshot or timer that\n// never happens in the real production build.\nReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById(\"root\")).render(<App />);\n";
export const PKG_TEST_README = "# NESR SLK Competency Framework System \u2014 standalone test package\n\nGenerated from the live running app on {DATE} by {USER}. No API, no SQL\ndatabase, no Azure \u2014 this is the fastest path to actually clicking through\nevery feature yourself.\n\n## Before you do anything else: add App.jsx\n\nThis package intentionally does NOT include `src/App.jsx`. A running web app\ncannot reliably read back its own original source code \u2014 there is no\nbrowser API for a page to introspect the exact source it was built from,\nespecially once bundled or transformed for display. Rather than attempt\nsomething fragile that could silently ship a stale or broken copy, this\npackage leaves that one file out on purpose.\n\n**Copy the App.jsx you already have** \u2014 the file downloadable throughout\nthis conversation as \"NESR SLK Competency System App\" \u2014 into:\n\n    src/App.jsx\n\nA minimal placeholder is there in the meantime so `npm install && npm run\ndev` succeeds even if you run it before doing this \u2014 it shows a plain\non-screen message telling you the same thing, rather than fail outright.\n\n## Run it\n\n```bash\nnpm install\nnpm run dev\n```\n\nOpen the URL Vite prints (usually http://localhost:5173). Sign in, click\nthrough every tab \u2014 Dashboard, Administration, Progression, Proficiency,\nMatrix, Tools \u2014 dark mode, responsive layout, exports, the works. Every\ncapability is here; only the multi-user sharing is not, by design.\n\n## What this genuinely gives you, and what it doesn't\n\n**Real:** every feature works exactly as in the source conversation. Data\npersists in this browser's IndexedDB \u2014 closing and reopening the tab keeps\nyour changes, restarting the browser keeps them, clearing browser data or\nswitching browsers does not.\n\n**Not included, on purpose:** this is one browser, one person. Two people\nrunning this package on two machines each get their own private,\ndisconnected dataset \u2014 there is no server tying them together. That's\nexactly the gap the full deployment package (also available from Data \u2192\nData & persistence in the app) closes, with a real Azure SQL backend and\nreal Entra sign-in. Use this package to actually test and demonstrate the\napp today; use that one when you're ready for a team to share it.\n\n## If `npm run build`/`npm run dev` fails\n\nTwo most common causes, in order of likelihood:\n1. **Node version** \u2014 this is tested against Node 20 (see `.nvmrc`); `nvm use`\n   if you have nvm.\n2. **A stale install** \u2014 `rm -rf node_modules package-lock.json && npm install`\n\nEvery dependency version here is pinned exactly (no `^` ranges) so what\ninstalls today matches what this was tested against.\n";
